          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            There are two big questions about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and the logical
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
          %ol
            %li What foundations are adequate to formulate category theory?
            %li How can category theory provide a foundation for mathematics?
          %p
            These questions also apply to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %h2#MFCT Mathematical foundations of category theory
          %p
            The problem with mathematical foundations of category theory is that in category theory we frequently speak of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theories
          %p
            One common view seems to be to found category theory on a theory of sets and classes; see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category_%28mathematics%29#Definition"} the English Wikipedia's definition
            = succeed "," do
              %em Categories for the Working Mathematician
            %em small
            and
            %em large
            categories. There is a category of all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small categories
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Alexander Grothendieck
            used more (although
            %a{:href => "http://permalink.gmane.org/gmane.science.mathematics.categories/6847"} apparently
            he did not need to); he used what we now call
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universes"} Grothendieck universes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is some universe) is
            = succeed "-large" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
