                    %mi x
                    %mo ×
                    %mi y
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leftarrow x \times y \rightarrow y
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctors
          %p
            For a discussion of set theoretic foundations of category theory see the references
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesSetTheoreticFoundationsOfCategoryTheory"} below
          %h3#Identity
            The concept of
            %em identity
          %p
            One way to think of category theory is as a framework in which the idea is formalized that every kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            is really secretly a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"} Michael Makkai
            works on a language,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FOLDS"} FOLDS
            (‘first-order logic with dependent sorts’), which is designed to make it impossible to formulate any statements that break the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %h2#CFM Categorial foundations of mathematics
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
            proposed to found mathematics on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCC"} ETCC
            (for ‘ Elementary Theory of the Category of Categories’), a first-order axiomatisation of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} the category of categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} the category of sets
            %em structural
            or
            %em categorial
            (or
            = succeed "," do
              %em categorical
          %p
            Lawvere's system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            (for ‘the Elementary Theory of the Category of Sets’) essentially states that the category of sets is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            with certain properties, in particular a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
            %em elementary
            topos that subsumes the original notion, now called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %p
            It is also possible to found mathematics on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal language
            of a topos. In this case, the topos need
            %em not
            be well-pointed (and indeed, the condition that a topos be well-pointed cannot be stated in its own internal language; or if you prefer,
            %em every
            topos is well-pointed
            = succeed ")." do
              %em internally
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
          %p
            Categories (not just toposes) can serve as models of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            (maybe
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            between categories and type theories.
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"} Paul Taylor
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Practical+Foundations"} Practical Foundations of Mathematics
            is essentially all about this subject, as is (at a more advanced level) most of the career of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"} Michael Makkai
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"} Jim Lambek
            proposed to use the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+topos"} free topos
            as ambient world to do mathematics in. Being syntactically constructed, but universally determined, with higher-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            he saw it as a reconciliation of the three classical schools of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy+of+mathematics"} philosophy of mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formalism"} formalism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/platonism"} platonism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionism"} intuitionism
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#LS11"} Lambek-Scott 2011
          %p
            Certain ‘strong’ axioms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            (those involving
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantification"} quantification
            over all sets) are difficult to state in category-theoretic (or type-theoretic) terms, but this can be overcome in a theory like ETCS; talk to Mike Shulman. (Ironically, this makes it harder to do foundations with categorial foundations!)
          %p
            In contrast, many of the optional or controversial axioms of set theory (such as the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %h2#CFCT Categorial foundations of category theory
          %p
            Relevant topics:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCC"} ETCC
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FOLDS"} FOLDS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %h2#related_topics Related topics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+foundations+of+mathematics"} univalent foundations of mathematics
          %h2#references  References
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Foundations and applications: axiomatization and education
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ucla.edu/~asl/bsl/0902/0902-006.ps"} ps
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.bsl/1052669290"} Euclid
          %p
            The following contains a careful discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%B6del%27s+incompleteness+theorem"} Gödel's incompleteness theorem
            in the context of categorical foundations using the free topos:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"}> Jim Lambek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Scott"} Phil Scott
              %em Reflections on Categorical Foundations of Mathematics
              , pp.171-185 in Sommaruga (ed.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Foundational Theories of Classical and Constructive Mathematics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.site.uottawa.ca/~phil/papers/LS11.final.pdf"} draft
          %p
            Some old discussions about category theory and foundations are archived
            %a{:href => "http://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/4172/foundation-of-mathematics/?Focus=33992#Comment_33992"} here
          %h3#ReferencesSetTheoreticFoundationsOfCategoryTheory Set theoretic foundations of category theory
          %p
            Without assuming
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universes"} Grothendieck universes
          %ul
            %li#Rao06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vidhyanath+K.+Rao"}> Vidhyanath K. Rao
